{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JSWRhoqp3yZYPP931kweLG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'ContamCal characterization framework fully validated. Cross-family\n2\t  Llama-3.2-3B replication confirms STEM gradient direction and ARC forgetting are\n3\t  benchmark×domain properties, not Qwen-specific artifacts. 7B scale bridging completes\n4\t  the scale modulation curve (catastrophic→negligible). Calibration conditionally\n5\t  effective: ARC and MMLU-STEM pass gating, GSM8K correctly gated out. IRT outperforms\n6\t  linear regression on ARC with bootstrap significance. QC-only d100 validates experimental\n7\t  design. 72B ecological validity. Closed-weight 2-signal mode is an honest negative\n8\t  result demonstrating S1-dependence. All 4 claims at full strength.\n9\t\n10\t  '\n11\timagined_experiments:\n12\t- name: sft_d100_qc_only\n13\t  plan_id: exp-015\n14\t  description: '100% dosage SFT using only 7547 QC-pass paraphrases (cosine>0.85,\n15\t    BLEU<0.3) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Compares against exp-001 d100-all (16697 items incl\n16\t    QC-fail). Isolates noisy-paraphrase confound on signal quality and IRT estimation.\n17\t\n18\t    '\n19\t  expected_results:\n20\t    sft_steps: 3650\n21\t    sft_final_loss: 1.555\n22\t    sft_token_accuracy: 0.672\n23\t    training_time_min: 60\n24\t    mmlu_accuracy: 0.261\n25\t    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.362\n26\t    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.305\n27\t    humaneval_pass1: 0.061\n28\t    mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.028\n29\t    gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.003\n30\t    arc_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.011\n31\t    s1_auc_qconly_d100_vs_d000: 0.69\n32\t    s1_auc_all_d100_vs_d000: 0.5\n33\t    s1_auc_improvement: 0.19\n34\t    gsm8k_gamma_qconly_refit: 0.08\n35\t    gsm8k_gamma_qconly_ci:\n36\t    - -0.85\n37\t    - 1.0\n38\t    gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n39\t    gsm8k_cal_mae_qconly: 0.032\n40\t    gsm8k_cal_mae_all: 0.037\n41\t  result_rationale: 'QC-only isolates two effects: signal quality and contamination\n42\t    purity.\n43\t\n44\t    (1) Signal AUC improvement: At d100-all, S1 AUC collapses to ~0.50 because ALL\n45\t    items are labeled \"contaminated\" but only ~47% (MMLU) to 60% (ARC) actually have\n46\t    high-quality paraphrases. The signal can''t discriminate because labels are noisy.\n47\t    QC-only removes the QC-fail items from both training data AND label set, so S1\n48\t    now compares d000 (genuinely clean) vs d100-QC-only (genuinely contaminated on\n49\t    the 7547 items). S1 AUC rises to ~0.69 — not 0.98 like d005 because at d100 the\n50\t    model''s representations shift globally (not just on contaminated items), but\n51\t    much better than d100-all''s labeling artifact.\n52\t\n53\t    (2) Accuracy differences: MMLU at 0.261 (vs d100-all 0.233) because fewer total\n54\t    contaminated items means less position bias amplification at 0.5B. The 7547 QC-pass\n55\t    items include 6596 MMLU items (47% of 14042) — still substantial MMLU contamination,\n56\t    but the 5446 QC-fail items that were adding noise/capacity displacement are removed.\n57\t    ARC at 0.305 (vs 0.294) similarly — 697/1172 QC-pass ARC items provide concentrated\n58\t    forgetting signal but less total capacity displacement. GSM8K essentially unchanged\n59\t    (0.362 vs 0.365) because only 228/1319 GSM8K items pass QC — negligible contamination\n60\t    in either condition.\n61\t\n62\t    (3) IRT impact: GSM8K γ remains null (0.08) because open-ended format resists\n63\t    contamination regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only condition mainly matters\n64\t    for MC benchmarks, but those are excluded from 0.5B IRT per D045.\n65\t\n66\t    Paper value: validates that QC filtering improves signal discriminability. Reported\n67\t    as supplementary evidence for experimental design robustness. The S1 AUC improvement\n68\t    (0.50 → 0.69) directly addresses Concern #14 (d100 labeling artifact) by showing\n69\t    the artifact is caused by noisy labels, not signal failure.\n70\t\n71\t    '\n72\t  tables:\n73\t  - name: 'Table S1: QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B d100)'\n74\t    columns:\n75\t    - Metric\n76\t    - d100-All\n77\t    - d100-QC-Only\n78\t    - Delta\n79\t    - Interpretation\n80\t    rows:\n81\t    - - MMLU Accuracy\n82\t      - 0.233\n83\t      - 0.261\n84\t      - 0.028\n85\t      - Less capacity displacement\n86\t    - - ARC Accuracy\n87\t      - 0.294\n88\t      - 0.305\n89\t      - 0.011\n90\t      - Reduced noisy SFT interference\n91\t    - - GSM8K Accuracy\n92\t      - 0.365\n93\t      - 0.362\n94\t      - -0.003\n95\t      - Null (minimal QC-pass GSM8K items)\n96\t    - - S1 AUC (d100 vs d000)\n97\t      - 0.5\n98\t      - 0.69\n99\t      - 0.19\n100\t      - Label noise reduction\n101\t    - - GSM8K Cal MAE\n102\t      - 0.037\n103\t      - 0.032\n104\t      - -0.005\n105\t      - Cleaner exposure → tighter IRT\n106\t  figures_description:\n107\t  - 'Supplementary: S1 score distribution histograms for d100-all vs d100-QC-only,\n108\t    showing cleaner bimodal separation in QC-only condition'\n109\t  analysis: 'Confirms the d100 AUC collapse (Concern #14) is a labeling artifact,\n110\t    not signal degradation. QC filtering partially restores discriminability. However,\n111\t    0.5B MC data remains excluded from main analysis per D045. Primary value is methodological\n112\t    validation and supplementary evidence for the controlled injection design.\n113\t\n114\t    '\n115\t- name: scale_bridging_7b\n116\t  plan_id: exp-007\n117\t  description: 'Qwen2.5-7B LoRA SFT at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100),\n118\t    1 seed. Extends scale modulation to 7B. Multi-GPU LoRA on H100 (~4-8h per run).\n119\t    Validates that contamination effects attenuate at larger scale.\n120\t\n121\t    '\n122\t  expected_results:\n123\t    sft_time_per_run_hours: 6.5\n124\t    total_gpu_hours: 32.5\n125\t    lora_adapter_size_mb: 210\n126\t    d000_mmlu: 0.724\n127\t    d000_gsm8k: 0.793\n128\t    d000_arc: 0.896\n129\t    d000_humaneval: 0.268\n130\t    d005_mmlu: 0.725\n131\t    d005_gsm8k: 0.791\n132\t    d005_arc: 0.895\n133\t    d005_humaneval: 0.274\n134\t    d025_mmlu: 0.726\n135\t    d025_gsm8k: 0.795\n136\t    d025_arc: 0.892\n137\t    d025_humaneval: 0.28\n138\t    d050_mmlu: 0.727\n139\t    d050_gsm8k: 0.796\n140\t    d050_arc: 0.89\n141\t    d050_humaneval: 0.287\n142\t    d100_mmlu: 0.729\n143\t    d100_gsm8k: 0.8\n144\t    d100_arc: 0.887\n145\t    d100_humaneval: 0.293\n146\t    mmlu_delta_d100: 0.005\n147\t    gsm8k_delta_d100: 0.007\n148\t    arc_delta_d100: -0.009\n149\t    humaneval_delta_d100: 0.025\n150\t    s1_d005_auc_7b: 0.97\n151\t    s2_d005_auc_7b: 0.51\n152\t    s2_d100_cohens_d_7b: -0.42\n153\t    s4_d005_auc_7b: 0.53\n154\t    arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.52\n155\t    arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci:\n156\t    - -0.12\n157\t    - 1.18\n158\t    arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n159\t    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl: 0.05\n160\t    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci:\n161\t    - -0.28\n162\t    - 0.38\n163\t    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n164\t    mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.58\n165\t    mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci:\n166\t    - -0.22\n167\t    - 1.4\n168\t    mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n169\t    coverage_95_arc_n24: 0.625\n170\t    coverage_95_gsm8k_n32: 0.688\n171\t    coverage_95_mmlu_n24: 0.667\n172\t    kendall_tau_arc_n24: 0.79\n173\t    kendall_tau_gsm8k_n32: 0.83\n174\t    scale_modulation:\n175\t      arc_05b: -17.5\n176\t      arc_15b: -0.7\n177\t      arc_3b: -0.3\n178\t      arc_7b: -0.9\n179\t      mmlu_05b: -16.5\n180\t      mmlu_15b: 0.8\n181\t      mmlu_3b: 0.8\n182\t      mmlu_7b: 0.5\n183\t      gsm8k_05b: 0.1\n184\t      gsm8k_15b: -0.5\n185\t      gsm8k_3b: 0.2\n186\t      gsm8k_7b: 0.7\n187\t  result_rationale: 'At 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that LoRA SFT contamination\n188\t    barely perturbs benchmark performance. The mechanism:\n189\t\n190\t    (1) MMLU shows <1pp change across dosages. The 7B model already encodes the factual\n191\t    knowledge tested by MMLU; re-exposure via SFT paraphrases is redundant. The STEM\n192\t    gradient (Math > Science >> CS_Eng) likely still exists at 7B but with attenuated\n193\t    magnitudes (STEM_Math γ might be +0.3 instead of +0.97 at 0.5B-3B), making domain-level\n194\t    γ harder to detect at 7B alone.\n195\t\n196\t    (2) ARC shows very mild forgetting (-0.9pp at d100). This is notable: the forgetting\n197\t    DIRECTION persists at 7B (negative delta), confirming ARC forgetting is a benchmark\n198\t    property not solely a small-model capacity artifact. But the magnitude is within\n199\t    noise for a single seed. The 2PL+γ pooled fit at N=24 (1.5B+3B+7B, D045 excludes\n200\t    0.5B) shows ARC γ=+0.52 — still positive because the very mild 7B forgetting is\n201\t    offset by 3B near-zero effects. The NEGATIVE ARC γ from best tier targets requires\n202\t    cross-family pooling (exp-014).\n203\t\n204\t    (3) GSM8K remains null (+0.7pp, within noise). Open-ended format resistance is\n205\t    scale-independent, as predicted. The pooled N=32 2PL+γ narrows the CI ([-0.28,\n206\t    0.38] vs [-0.36, 0.49] at N=24), further confirming the null.\n207\t\n208\t    (4) HumanEval shows moderate dose-response (+2.5pp at d100). At 7B, code generation\n209\t    benefits from exposure to coding patterns in contamination data, but less dramatically\n210\t    than at 1.5B (+17.7pp). This suggests HumanEval contamination sensitivity is also\n211\t    scale-modulated but in the opposite direction from ARC — consistent with the \"benchmark-dependent\n212\t    direction\" framing.\n213\t\n214\t    (5) Signal behavior: S1 remains reliable (AUC=0.97) because embedding similarity\n215\t    is architecture- and scale-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ reversal attenuates at 7B (Cohen''s\n216\t    d = -0.42 vs -1.98 at 0.5B) — larger models have more distributed representations,\n217\t    so perplexity-based signals are less directionally misleading but still near-chance\n218\t    for discrimination. S4 Self-Critique shows marginal improvement (0.53 vs 0.48\n219\t    at 0.5B) — larger models can better self-assess, but the effect is modest.\n220\t\n221\t    (6) Coverage: Increasing N from 16→24 (ARC/MMLU) and 24→32 (GSM8K) provides moderate\n222\t    improvement. GSM8K at N=32 reaches 0.688, approaching the best tier target of\n223\t    0.60-0.75. ARC remains at 0.625 due to model specification issues (2PL+γ positive\n224\t    γ). The best tier''s coverage_95_at_n24 ∈ [0.60, 0.75] is met by GSM8K and MMLU\n225\t    but ARC needs cross-family data to resolve the γ sign.\n226\t\n227\t    '\n228\t  tables:\n229\t  - name: 'Table 3: Scale Modulation — Accuracy Delta (d100 − d000)'\n230\t    columns:\n231\t    - Benchmark\n232\t    - 0.5B\n233\t    - 1.5B\n234\t    - 3B\n235\t    - 7B\n236\t    rows:\n237\t    - - MMLU*\n238\t      - -16.5pp\n239\t      - +0.8pp\n240\t      - +0.8pp\n241\t      - +0.5pp\n242\t    - - ARC\n243\t      - -17.5pp\n244\t      - -0.7pp\n245\t      - -0.3pp\n246\t      - -0.9pp\n247\t    - - GSM8K\n248\t      - +0.1pp\n249\t      - -0.5pp\n250\t      - +0.2pp\n251\t      - +0.7pp\n252\t    - - HumanEval\n253\t      - -11.0pp\n254\t      - +17.7pp\n255\t      - +0.6pp\n256\t      - +2.5pp\n257\t    note: '* 0.5B MMLU/ARC excluded from IRT (D045 position bias). ARC shows consistent\n258\t      negative direction across all scales. GSM8K within noise at all scales. HumanEval\n259\t      peaks at 1.5B.\n260\t\n261\t      '\n262\t  - name: 'Table 4a: Pooled IRT 2PL+γ Results (Qwen-only, with 7B)'\n263\t    columns:\n264\t    - Benchmark\n265\t    - N\n266\t    - γ̂\n267\t    - 95% CI\n268\t    - Excl 0?\n269\t    - PPP\n270\t    - raw_MAE\n271\t    - cal_MAE\n272\t    rows:\n273\t    - - ARC\n274\t      - 24\n275\t      - 0.52\n276\t      - '[-0.12, 1.18]'\n277\t      - 'No'\n278\t      - 0.015\n279\t      - 0.032\n280\t      - 0.018\n281\t    - - GSM8K\n282\t      - 32\n283\t      - 0.05\n284\t      - '[-0.28, 0.38]'\n285\t      - 'No'\n286\t      - 0.52\n287\t      - 0.155\n288\t      - 0.005\n289\t    - - MMLU\n290\t      - 24\n291\t      - 0.58\n292\t      - '[-0.22, 1.40]'\n293\t      - 'No'\n294\t      - 0.21\n295\t      - 0.014\n296\t      - 0.006\n297\t    note: 'ARC γ positive in Qwen-only pool (D045 excludes 0.5B forgetting). Cross-family\n298\t      pooling (exp-014) needed for negative ARC γ per best tier.\n299\t\n300\t      '\n301\t  figures_description:\n302\t  - 'Figure 3: Dose-response curves across 4 scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B) for ARC, MMLU,\n303\t    GSM8K — ARC converges to flat at ≥1.5B, GSM8K flat at all scales'\n304\t  - 'Figure S2: Signal AUC heatmap extended to 7B — S1 stable, S2 reversal attenuates'\n305\t  - 'Figure S3: 7B dose-response for all 4 benchmarks (near-flat lines confirming\n306\t    scale modulation endpoint for MC)'\n307\t  analysis: '7B completes the Qwen-only scale modulation story. Key contribution:\n308\t    ARC forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (raw delta -0.9pp) even though the pooled\n309\t    IRT γ is positive when 0.5B is excluded. This is resolved in exp-014: Llama cross-family\n310\t    data where 3B may show stronger ARC forgetting, pulling pooled γ negative. The\n311\t    7B data''s primary value is (a) validating negligible MC effects at scale, (b)\n312\t    HumanEval dose-response at scale, (c) increasing pooled N for tighter CIs and\n313\t    better coverage, (d) confirming S1 reliability and S2 reversal attenuation at\n314\t    scale.\n315\t\n316\t    '\n317\t- name: multi_family_validation\n318\t  plan_id: exp-014\n319\t  description: 'Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100),\n320\t    1 seed. Validates cross-family generalization of domain heterogeneity, ARC forgetting,\n321\t    and GSM8K resistance. Critical for establishing contamination susceptibility as\n322\t    benchmark×domain property.\n323\t\n324\t    '\n325\t  expected_results:\n326\t    sft_time_per_run_hours: 2.3\n327\t    total_gpu_hours: 6.9\n328\t    llama_d000_mmlu: 0.455\n329\t    llama_d000_gsm8k: 0.481\n330\t    llama_d000_arc: 0.742\n331\t    llama_d000_humaneval: 0.195\n332\t    llama_d025_mmlu: 0.459\n333\t    llama_d025_gsm8k: 0.488\n334\t    llama_d025_arc: 0.721\n335\t    llama_d025_humaneval: 0.207\n336\t    llama_d100_mmlu: 0.463\n337\t    llama_d100_gsm8k: 0.495\n338\t    llama_d100_arc: 0.695\n339\t    llama_d100_humaneval: 0.22\n340\t    llama_arc_delta: -0.047\n341\t    llama_mmlu_delta: 0.008\n342\t    llama_gsm8k_delta: 0.014\n343\t    llama_humaneval_delta: 0.025\n344\t    llama_s1_d005_auc: 0.96\n345\t    llama_s2_d005_auc: 0.49\n346\t    llama_s2_d100_cohens_d: -1.45\n347\t    llama_s4_d005_auc: 0.5\n348\t    llama_arc_gamma: -1.65\n349\t    llama_arc_gamma_ci:\n350\t    - -3.8\n351\t    - 0.5\n352\t    llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n353\t    llama_gsm8k_gamma: 0.12\n354\t    llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n355\t    - -1.3\n356\t    - 1.5\n357\t    llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n358\t    llama_stem_math_gamma: 0.73\n359\t    llama_stem_math_ci:\n360\t    - 0.08\n361\t    - 1.42\n362\t    llama_stem_math_ci_excludes_zero: true\n363\t    llama_stem_science_gamma: 0.56\n364\t    llama_stem_science_ci:\n365\t    - -0.12\n366\t    - 1.25\n367\t    llama_stem_science_ci_excludes_zero: false\n368\t    llama_cs_eng_gamma: 0.08\n369\t    llama_cs_eng_ci:\n370\t    - -0.65\n371\t    - 0.82\n372\t    llama_cs_eng_ci_excludes_zero: false\n373\t    stem_gradient_direction_matches_qwen: true\n374\t    arc_forgetting_direction_matches_qwen: true\n375\t    gsm8k_null_matches_qwen: true\n376\t    pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl: -1.18\n377\t    pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci:\n378\t    - -2.25\n379\t    - -0.15\n380\t    pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci_excludes_zero: true\n381\t    pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma: 0.08\n382\t    pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n383\t    - -0.22\n384\t    - 0.38\n385\t    pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n386\t    pooled_cross_family_n_models: 30\n387\t    coverage_95_cross_family_arc: 0.67\n388\t    coverage_95_cross_family_gsm8k: 0.7\n389\t    kendall_tau_cross_family: 0.82\n390\t    irt_mae_arc: 0.028\n391\t    linear_reg_mae_arc: 0.048\n392\t    bootstrap_p_irt_vs_linear: 0.032\n393\t    bootstrap_n: 5000\n394\t    benchmarks_passing_gating: 2\n395\t    gating_pass_arc: true\n396\t    gating_pass_mmlu_stem: true\n397\t    gating_fail_gsm8k: true\n398\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-family validation is the most consequential planned experiment.\n399\t    It elevates the characterization framework from \"Qwen-specific observation\" to\n400\t    \"general benchmark property.\" Here''s why each result is expected:\n401\t\n402\t    (1) Llama-3.2-3B ARC forgetting (-4.7pp at d100): Llama-3.2-3B has a lower ARC\n403\t    baseline (0.742 vs Qwen2.5-3B 0.824) and a different architecture (grouped-query\n404\t    attention, different tokenizer). The lower baseline means the model has less headroom\n405\t    — SFT contamination competes with limited capacity for science reasoning, producing\n406\t    more pronounced forgetting than Qwen2.5-3B (-0.3pp). This is the critical mechanism:\n407\t    at equivalent parameter count, models with less task-specific capacity show stronger\n408\t    forgetting, and the forgetting DIRECTION is preserved regardless of architecture.\n409\t    The raw -4.7pp delta provides direct evidence for ARC forgetting as a benchmark\n410\t    property.\n411\t\n412\t    (2) Llama-only IRT (N=6): γ=-1.65 but wide CI crossing zero. N=6 is too small\n413\t    for significance — this is expected from exp-004 simulations showing N≥50 for\n414\t    reliable recovery. However, the point estimate''s SIGN is negative, directionally\n415\t    consistent with Qwen results. The value is the sign, not the significance.\n416\t\n417\t    (3) Pooled cross-family 4PL+γ (N=30): This is where best tier is achieved. Adding\n418\t    Llama data (with genuine ARC forgetting) to Qwen data (with near-flat ARC at 1.5B+/3B)\n419\t    produces a negative pooled γ=-1.18 because the Llama points pull the estimate\n420\t    negative while 7B and 3B Qwen points sit near zero. The 4PL+γ model (appropriate\n421\t    for MCQ) with S1-only exposure (the reliable signal) produces CI=[-2.25, -0.15]\n422\t    that excludes zero. This is within the best tier target range of [-2.5, -0.8].\n423\t\n424\t    (4) STEM gradient replication: Llama STEM_Math γ=0.73 (Qwen: 0.97) — same positive\n425\t    direction, slightly smaller magnitude. The gradient Math > Science > CS_Eng is\n426\t    preserved: 0.73 > 0.56 > 0.08. Math CI excludes zero; Science borderline; CS_Eng\n427\t    null. The direction match across families (both showing Math > Science >> CS_Eng\n428\t    ≈ 0) is strong evidence that contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge\n429\t    density, a property of the benchmark items, not the model. Lower Llama magnitudes\n430\t    reflect: (a) different pre-training distribution (less MMLU-adjacent data in Llama''s\n431\t    pre-training), (b) different SFT dynamics (Llama''s architecture processes paraphrases\n432\t    differently), but (c) the same underlying susceptibility gradient determined by\n433\t    item characteristics.\n434\t\n435\t    (5) GSM8K null replication: γ=0.12, CI firmly crossing zero. Open-ended format\n436\t    resistance is architecture-independent because the mechanism is format-driven:\n437\t    chain-of-thought generation cannot be boosted by memorizing answer templates.\n438\t    This holds across model families.\n439\t\n440\t    (6) Signal behavior: S1 embedding AUC=0.96 (Llama) — sentence-transformer similarity\n441\t    is model-agnostic, confirming S1''s robustness. S2 Min-K++ reversal replicates\n442\t    (Cohen''s d=-1.45) because the reversal is driven by small-model memorization\n443\t    dynamics that alter perplexity in the opposite-to-expected direction, a phenomenon\n444\t    shared across architectures at comparable scale.\n445\t\n446\t    (7) IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap p=0.032): With cross-family N=30 and the\n447\t    4PL+γ model, ARC IRT MAE=0.028 vs linear regression MAE=0.048. The bootstrap test\n448\t    is significant because IRT captures the nonlinear relationship between ability,\n449\t    contamination, and item difficulty that linear regression misses — specifically,\n450\t    the c/d parameters of 4PL model the guessing and ceiling effects in MCQ that produce\n451\t    heteroscedastic residuals. At N=30, the IRT advantage is large enough to be statistically\n452\t    distinguishable.\n453\t\n454\t    (8) Calibration gating: ARC passes (γ significant, PPP in range, cal MAE < raw\n455\t    MAE). MMLU-STEM domains pass at the domain level (STEM_Math γ significant across\n456\t    both families). GSM8K correctly gated out (γ null). This yields 2 benchmarks (of\n457\t    3 main) passing gating, within the best tier target of 2-3.\n458\t\n459\t    '\n460\t  tables:\n461\t  - name: 'Table 5: Cross-Family MMLU Domain γ Comparison'\n462\t    columns:\n463\t    - Domain\n464\t    - Qwen γ̂ (N=23)\n465\t    - Llama γ̂ (N=6)\n466\t    - Direction Match\n467\t    - Interpretation\n468\t    rows:\n469\t    - - STEM_Math\n470\t      - +0.97 [0.53,1.43]\n471\t      - +0.73 [0.08,1.42]\n472\t      - ✓ both positive\n473\t      - Factual recall — both susceptible\n474\t    - - STEM_Science\n475\t      - +0.84 [0.39,1.27]\n476\t      - +0.56 [−0.12,1.25]\n477\t      - ✓ both positive*\n478\t      - Science facts — susceptible, Llama borderline\n479\t    - - STEM_CS_Eng\n480\t      - +0.20 [−0.45,0.84]\n481\t      - +0.08 [−0.65,0.82]\n482\t      - ✓ both null\n483\t      - Procedural — resistant\n484\t    - - Medical\n485\t      - −0.20 PPP=1.0\n486\t      - −0.15 PPP=1.0\n487\t      - ✓ both misfit\n488\t      - 2PL+γ scope boundary\n489\t    - - Humanities\n490\t      - −0.41 PPP=1.0\n491\t      - −0.30 PPP=1.0\n492\t      - ✓ both misfit\n493\t      - 2PL+γ scope boundary\n494\t    note: '* Llama STEM_Science CI borderline includes zero at N=6.'\n495\t  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Family Benchmark γ Summary'\n496\t    columns:\n497\t    - Benchmark\n498\t    - Qwen-only 2PL+γ (N=24)\n499\t    - Llama-only 2PL+γ (N=6)\n500\t    - Pooled 4PL+γ S1 (N=30)\n501\t    - Direction\n502\t    rows:\n503\t    - - ARC\n504\t      - +0.52 [−0.12,1.18]\n505\t      - −1.65 [−3.8,0.5]\n506\t      - −1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]*\n507\t      - Forgetting (benchmark)\n508\t    - - GSM8K\n509\t      - +0.05 [−0.28,0.38]\n510\t      - +0.12 [−1.3,1.5]\n511\t      - +0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\n512\t      - Null (format)\n513\t    - - MMLU\n514\t      - +0.58 [−0.22,1.40]\n515\t      - +0.35 [−0.8,1.5]\n516\t      - +0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\n517\t      - Domain-dependent\n518\t    note: '* ARC γ significant only in pooled cross-family 4PL+γ — this is the primary\n519\t      specification for MCQ.'\n520\t  - name: 'Table 7: IRT vs Linear Regression (ARC, N=30 cross-family)'\n521\t    columns:\n522\t    - Method\n523\t    - MAE\n524\t    - 95% Bootstrap CI\n525\t    - p vs Linear\n526\t    rows:\n527\t    - - Raw (no adjustment)\n528\t      - 0.055\n529\t      - '[0.038, 0.072]'\n530\t      - —\n531\t    - - B4 Linear Regression\n532\t      - 0.048\n533\t      - '[0.032, 0.064]'\n534\t      - —\n535\t    - - ContamCal 4PL+γ\n536\t      - 0.028\n537\t      - '[0.015, 0.041]'\n538\t      - p=0.032\n539\t    - - B3 Vanilla 4PL\n540\t      - 0.062\n541\t      - '[0.043, 0.081]'\n542\t      - p=0.92\n543\t    - - B6 DCR Fuzzy\n544\t      - 0.185\n545\t      - '[0.140, 0.230]'\n546\t      - p<0.001\n547\t  figures_description:\n548\t  - 'Figure 5: Paired forest plot — domain-level γ for Qwen vs Llama, showing directional\n549\t    consistency. STEM domains (Math, Science) positive; non-STEM PPP=1.0 boundary.\n550\t    Error bars overlap but signs match.'\n551\t  - 'Figure 6: Cross-family ARC dose-response — Llama shows clearer negative slope\n552\t    than Qwen at 3B, converging evidence for ARC forgetting as benchmark property.'\n553\t  - 'Figure S4: MMLU STEM gradient visualization (γ vs domain) for both families on\n554\t    same axes, showing parallel gradients at different magnitudes.'\n555\t  analysis: 'This experiment is the linchpin of the best tier. It resolves the ARC\n556\t    γ sign tension: Qwen-only (D045-compliant, excluding 0.5B) shows positive γ because\n557\t    Qwen2.5-3B is barely affected on ARC; Llama-3.2-3B shows genuine forgetting (-4.7pp),\n558\t    pulling the cross-family pooled γ negative. The 4PL+γ specification (appropriate\n559\t    for MCQ) captures the guessing dynamics that 2PL misses. At N=30 cross-family,\n560\t    ARC γ CI excludes zero at [-2.25, -0.15], meeting best tier targets.\n561\t\n562\t    The STEM gradient replication across families is the strongest evidence for the\n563\t    characterization framework: contamination susceptibility is determined by item-level\n564\t    properties (factual knowledge density), not model-level properties (architecture,\n565\t    tokenizer, pre-training data). Two families, same gradient direction, same benchmark-dependent\n566\t    effects = benchmark×domain property established.\n567\t\n568\t    IRT vs linear regression achieves statistical significance (p=0.032) because N=30\n569\t    provides sufficient power and the 4PL nonlinear modeling of MCQ guessing/ceiling\n570\t    effects captures variance that linear regression cannot. This addresses Concern\n571\t    #21.\n572\t\n573\t    '\n574\t- name: rl_observational_case_study\n575\t  plan_id: exp-009\n576\t  description: 'Apply ContamCal pipeline to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct\n577\t    (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill (1.5B/7B). Signal computation, IRT fitting\n578\t    with wide priors, expected regret quantification, and near-zero γ specificity\n579\t    check on known-clean benchmark.\n580\t\n581\t    '\n582\t  expected_results:\n583\t    instruct_05b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.58\n584\t    instruct_05b_s1_auc_arc: 0.55\n585\t    instruct_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.61\n586\t    instruct_15b_s1_auc_arc: 0.6\n587\t    instruct_3b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.63\n588\t    instruct_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.65\n589\t    deepseek_r1_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.57\n590\t    deepseek_r1_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.62\n591\t    instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_mmlu: 0.078\n592\t    instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_arc: 0.031\n593\t    obs_arc_gamma: -0.85\n594\t    obs_arc_gamma_ci:\n595\t    - -2.1\n596\t    - 0.4\n597\t    obs_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n598\t    obs_gsm8k_gamma: 0.18\n599\t    obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n600\t    - -0.35\n601\t    - 0.72\n602\t    obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n603\t    obs_mmlu_gamma: 0.42\n604\t    obs_mmlu_gamma_ci:\n605\t    - -0.25\n606\t    - 1.1\n607\t    regret_uncalibrated_pp: 3.4\n608\t    regret_calibrated_pp: 1.9\n609\t    regret_remove_rescore_pp: 2.8\n610\t    regret_reduction_pct: 44.1\n611\t    regret_n_candidates: 12\n612\t    regret_top_k: 5\n613\t    regret_ground_truth: held-out clean benchmark (not calibrated scores)\n614\t    clean_benchmark_name: WinoGrande\n615\t    clean_gamma: 0.04\n616\t    clean_gamma_ci:\n617\t    - -0.55\n618\t    - 0.62\n619\t    clean_gamma_ci_includes_zero: true\n620\t    specificity_confirmed: true\n621\t    coverage_95_obs: 0.53\n622\t    coverage_95_controlled_only: 0.67\n623\t    deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_15b: 0.863\n624\t    deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_7b: 0.924\n625\t    deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_15b: 0.542\n626\t    deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_7b: 0.718\n627\t  result_rationale: 'Observational RL analysis necessarily produces wider CIs and\n628\t    weaker γ estimates. The key contributions are directional consistency, practical\n629\t    utility, and specificity validation.\n630\t\n631\t    (1) Signal degradation: S1 AUC drops from 0.95-0.99 (controlled SFT) to 0.55-0.65\n632\t    (observational RL). This is expected because: (a) we don''t know which items these\n633\t    models saw during training, so S1''s reference database (original benchmark items)\n634\t    may not match the actual contamination source, (b) RL post-training (RLHF/DPO)\n635\t    modifies representations beyond what SFT alone does, reducing embedding similarity\n636\t    between model outputs and the benchmark items we check against, (c) Instruct models\n637\t    are further from base than our controlled SFT models, making S1 similarity scores\n638\t    noisier. S4 Self-Critique shows the RL-sensitivity found in exp-008 (delta ~0.08\n639\t    on MMLU), confirming that S4 differentiates SFT from RL to a small degree.\n640\t\n641\t    (2) IRT γ directions: ARC γ=-0.85 (negative, directionally consistent with controlled\n642\t    results). The magnitude is attenuated because observational exposure scores are\n643\t    noisier. CI includes zero because N_observational is small and signal quality\n644\t    is lower. GSM8K γ=0.18 (null, consistent). MMLU γ=0.42 (positive direction, consistent\n645\t    with STEM inflation). The CIs are uniformly wider than controlled experiments\n646\t    — this is honest and expected.\n647\t\n648\t    (3) Expected regret: Even with noisy signals, calibrated ranking reduces expected\n649\t    regret by 44% for top-5 model selection. The mechanism: IRT calibration correctly\n650\t    adjusts the ranking of models with high exposure scores (likely contaminated)\n651\t    relative to models with low exposure. Even with wide CIs, the DIRECTION of adjustment\n652\t    is correct often enough to improve selection. Ground truth = held-out clean benchmark\n653\t    (WinoGrande) scores, NOT calibrated scores, avoiding circularity (Concern #12).\n654\t    The 44% reduction is meaningful for practitioners: choosing among top-5 models\n655\t    with calibrated vs uncalibrated ranking avoids ~1.5pp expected contamination bias.\n656\t\n657\t    (4) Specificity: WinoGrande γ=0.04, CI firmly crossing zero. This benchmark is\n658\t    not in standard contamination pathways (sentence completion format, less likely\n659\t    to appear in training data). The near-zero γ confirms ContamCal doesn''t hallucinate\n660\t    contamination effects — it produces null results when contamination is absent.\n661\t    This addresses the implicit concern that IRT γ might absorb any model variation,\n662\t    not specifically contamination.\n663\t\n664\t    (5) DeepSeek-R1-Distill: These models show high GSM8K accuracy (0.863-0.924) potentially\n665\t    reflecting distillation from R1''s chain-of-thought, which may encode benchmark-specific\n666\t    patterns. However, without controlled injection, we cannot distinguish genuine\n667\t    capability from contamination. The IRT framework assigns wide CIs, correctly reflecting\n668\t    this uncertainty.\n669\t\n670\t    (6) Coverage at 0.53 (observational pooled): Lower than controlled-only (0.67)\n671\t    because observational data introduces heterogeneity that the IRT model doesn''t\n672\t    fully capture. This is reported honestly as a limitation of observational extension.\n673\t\n674\t    '\n675\t  tables:\n676\t  - name: 'Table 8: Expected Regret — Top-5 Model Selection (N=12 candidates)'\n677\t    columns:\n678\t    - Strategy\n679\t    - Expected Regret (pp)\n680\t    - vs Uncalibrated\n681\t    - Description\n682\t    rows:\n683\t    - - Uncalibrated (raw ranking)\n684\t      - 3.4\n685\t      - —\n686\t      - Rank by raw benchmark scores\n687\t    - - Remove-and-rescore\n688\t      - 2.8\n689\t      - −17.6%\n690\t      - Drop flagged items, re-rank\n691\t    - - ContamCal (calibrated)\n692\t      - 1.9\n693\t      - −44.1%\n694\t      - IRT-adjusted scores, re-rank\n695\t    - - Oracle (ground truth)\n696\t      - 0.0\n697\t      - −100%\n698\t      - Perfect clean scores\n699\t  - name: 'Table S7: Observational vs Controlled γ Comparison'\n700\t    columns:\n701\t    - Benchmark\n702\t    - Controlled γ̂ (N=30)\n703\t    - w/ Observational γ̂ (N≈38)\n704\t    - Direction Consistent\n705\t    rows:\n706\t    - - ARC\n707\t      - −1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]\n708\t      - −0.85 [−2.1,0.4]\n709\t      - ✓ (attenuated, CI wider)\n710\t    - - GSM8K\n711\t      - +0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\n712\t      - +0.18 [−0.35,0.72]\n713\t      - ✓ (null preserved)\n714\t    - - MMLU\n715\t      - +0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\n716\t      - +0.42 [−0.25,1.10]\n717\t      - ✓ (positive preserved)\n718\t  - name: 'Table S8: Specificity Check — Known-Clean Benchmark'\n719\t    columns:\n720\t    - Benchmark\n721\t    - γ̂\n722\t    - 95% CI\n723\t    - Includes 0?\n724\t    - Interpretation\n725\t    rows:\n726\t    - - WinoGrande\n727\t      - 0.04\n728\t      - '[−0.55, 0.62]'\n729\t      - 'Yes'\n730\t      - No false contamination detection\n731\t  figures_description:\n732\t  - 'Figure 7: Expected regret curve — regret vs number of selected models (top-k)\n733\t    for 3 strategies, showing consistent calibration advantage across k=3,5,7,10.'\n734\t  - 'Figure S5: Observational γ forest plot overlaid on controlled γ estimates. Direction\n735\t    consistent, CIs wider. Gray band = controlled 95% CI.'\n736\t  analysis: 'The RL observational case study provides three key contributions: (1)\n737\t    directional consistency confirms the characterization framework generalizes beyond\n738\t    controlled SFT, (2) expected regret reduction demonstrates practical value even\n739\t    with noisy signals, (3) specificity check validates that ContamCal produces null\n740\t    results on clean benchmarks. The wider CIs and non-significance of individual\n741\t    γ estimates are honest — we frame this as \"directional external validity\" not\n742\t    \"quantitative confirmation.\" DeepSeek-R1 results motivate future work on RL-contamination\n743\t    interaction. Coverage degradation (0.67→0.53) when adding observational data is\n744\t    a limitation, attributed to heterogeneity from mixing controlled and observational\n745\t    designs.\n746\t\n747\t    '\n748\t- name: observational_72b\n749\t  plan_id: exp-012\n750\t  description: 'Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Compute signals\n751\t    and benchmark evaluations; add as single high-ability anchor point in pooled IRT.\n752\t    No SFT. Extends ecological validity to frontier scale.\n753\t\n754\t    '\n755\t  expected_results:\n756\t    mmlu_accuracy: 0.848\n757\t    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.916\n758\t    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.944\n759\t    humaneval_pass1: 0.628\n760\t    s1_exposure_mmlu: 0.11\n761\t    s1_exposure_arc: 0.07\n762\t    s1_exposure_gsm8k: 0.14\n763\t    s2_minkpp_mmlu_mean: 1.69\n764\t    s2_minkpp_arc_mean: 1.68\n765\t    s4_selfcritique_mmlu_entropy: 2.35\n766\t    pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma: -1.15\n767\t    pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma_ci:\n768\t    - -2.2\n769\t    - -0.12\n770\t    pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma: 0.07\n771\t    pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n772\t    - -0.21\n773\t    - 0.36\n774\t    pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma: 0.5\n775\t    pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma_ci:\n776\t    - -0.16\n777\t    - 1.18\n778\t    pooled_with_72b_n: 31\n779\t    arc_72b_raw: 0.944\n780\t    arc_72b_calibrated: 0.948\n781\t    arc_72b_delta: 0.004\n782\t    mmlu_72b_raw: 0.848\n783\t    mmlu_72b_calibrated: 0.843\n784\t    mmlu_72b_delta: -0.005\n785\t    gsm8k_72b_raw: 0.916\n786\t    gsm8k_72b_calibrated: 0.916\n787\t    gsm8k_72b_delta: 0.0\n788\t    coverage_95_with_72b: 0.68\n789\t    kendall_tau_with_72b: 0.84\n790\t    inference_gpu_hours: 12\n791\t  result_rationale: 'At 72B, the model is near ceiling on most benchmarks, and contamination\n792\t    effects — if any exist from pre-training data overlap — are undetectable.\n793\t\n794\t    (1) Accuracy near ceiling: 72B achieves 84.8% MMLU, 94.4% ARC, 91.6% GSM8K. These\n795\t    are near the practical ceiling for these benchmarks (human performance is ~89.8%\n796\t    MMLU, ~80% GSM8K-hard). At this performance level, any contamination inflation\n797\t    is small relative to genuine capability.\n798\t\n799\t    (2) Low exposure scores: S1 exposure scores are 0.07-0.14 (vs 0.0-0.20 range in\n800\t    controlled SFT models). The 72B model''s training data likely includes some benchmark-adjacent\n801\t    material through natural web crawl, producing mild S1 similarity. But these scores\n802\t    are comparable to d000 (clean) controlled models, so the IRT model correctly treats\n803\t    72B as a low-exposure data point.\n804\t\n805\t    (3) Negligible calibration deltas: ARC +0.4pp (mild positive correction from the\n806\t    forgetting model — the IRT model slightly \"un-forgets\" for 72B, consistent with\n807\t    the negative γ meaning lower-exposure models have slightly higher true ability\n808\t    than observed). MMLU -0.5pp (mild STEM inflation correction consistent with positive\n809\t    MMLU γ). GSM8K unchanged (null γ means no adjustment).\n810\t\n811\t    (4) Value as high-θ anchor: The IRT model benefits from a wider ability distribution\n812\t    (0.5B low-ability through 72B high-ability). The 72B data point helps anchor item\n813\t    difficulty parameters at the high end, improving estimation precision for all\n814\t    models. This is analogous to including very high-ability examinees in educational\n815\t    testing — they provide information about difficult items that lower-ability examinees\n816\t    cannot. Kendall''s τ improves from 0.82 to 0.84 due to better separation at the\n817\t    high end of the ability distribution.\n818\t\n819\t    (5) Coverage marginal improvement: 0.67→0.68 (adding 1 data point to N≈30 has\n820\t    minimal impact on coverage). The value is not statistical power but ecological\n821\t    validity at frontier scale.\n822\t\n823\t    (6) Inference cost: ~12 GPU-hours for evaluating 72B on 4 benchmarks plus computing\n824\t    3 signals on all items. Dominated by S2 Min-K++ which requires full forward passes\n825\t    on ~16K items.\n826\t\n827\t    '\n828\t  tables:\n829\t  - name: 'Table S9: 72B Observational Calibration'\n830\t    columns:\n831\t    - Benchmark\n832\t    - Raw Accuracy\n833\t    - Calibrated\n834\t    - Δ (pp)\n835\t    - Interpretation\n836\t    rows:\n837\t    - - MMLU\n838\t      - 0.848\n839\t      - 0.843\n840\t      - −0.5\n841\t      - Mild STEM inflation correction\n842\t    - - ARC\n843\t      - 0.944\n844\t      - 0.948\n845\t      - '+0.4'\n846\t      - Forgetting model slight upward correction\n847\t    - - GSM8K\n848\t      - 0.916\n849\t      - 0.916\n850\t      - '0.0'\n851\t      - Null γ → no adjustment\n852\t    - - HumanEval\n853\t      - 0.628\n854\t      - 0.628\n855\t      - '0.0'\n856\t      - Not calibrated (appendix-only)\n857\t  figures_description:\n858\t  - 'Figure S6: Calibrated-vs-raw accuracy scatter across all scales (0.5B to 72B).\n859\t    72B cluster on the diagonal — minimal correction. Dashed line = identity. Lower\n860\t    scales show larger deviations.'\n861\t  - 'Inset in Figure 3: 72B point added to dose-response plot at d=unknown, positioned\n862\t    at observed accuracy with wide CI bar.'\n863\t  analysis: '72B confirms the scale modulation endpoint: at frontier scale, contamination\n864\t    effects vanish for all practical purposes. The sub-1pp calibration deltas are\n865\t    within evaluation noise. The contribution is ecological validity (\"the framework\n866\t    applies from 0.5B to 72B\") and the high-θ anchor effect on IRT estimation. Paper\n867\t    frames as: \"At frontier scales, raw benchmark scores are already reliable estimates\n868\t    of true ability — ContamCal''s value concentrates at smaller scales where contamination\n869\t    effects are measurable.\"\n870\t\n871\t    '\n872\t- name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n873\t  plan_id: exp-013\n874\t  description: 'Ablation: 2-signal closed-weight mode using only ConStat (S3, performance\n875\t    gap between original and paraphrased items) and Self-Critique (S4, entropy collapse).\n876\t    No embeddings (S1) or logprobs (S2 Min-K++). Quantifies calibration degradation\n877\t    when only API-level signals are available.\n878\t\n879\t    '\n880\t  expected_results:\n881\t    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mmlu: 0.52\n882\t    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_arc: 0.53\n883\t    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_gsm8k: 0.51\n884\t    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.52\n885\t    rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.68\n886\t    rrf_s1only_auc_d005_mean: 0.98\n887\t    arc_gamma_2signal: -0.48\n888\t    arc_gamma_2signal_ci:\n889\t    - -2.4\n890\t    - 1.5\n891\t    arc_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n892\t    gsm8k_gamma_2signal: 0.06\n893\t    gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci:\n894\t    - -1.0\n895\t    - 1.1\n896\t    gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n897\t    mmlu_gamma_2signal: 0.28\n898\t    mmlu_gamma_2signal_ci:\n899\t    - -1.2\n900\t    - 1.8\n901\t    arc_mae_2signal: 0.049\n902\t    arc_mae_4signal_s1only: 0.028\n903\t    arc_mae_raw: 0.055\n904\t    gsm8k_mae_2signal: 0.012\n905\t    gsm8k_mae_raw: 0.006\n906\t    mmlu_mae_2signal: 0.014\n907\t    mmlu_mae_raw: 0.014\n908\t    coverage_95_2signal_arc: 0.4\n909\t    coverage_95_2signal_gsm8k: 0.47\n910\t    coverage_95_4signal_arc: 0.67\n911\t    coverage_95_4signal_gsm8k: 0.7\n912\t    kendall_tau_2signal: 0.55\n913\t    kendall_tau_4signal: 0.82\n914\t    benchmarks_passing_gating_2signal: 0\n915\t    gating_fail_reason_arc: γ CI includes zero\n916\t    gating_fail_reason_gsm8k: γ CI includes zero (expected)\n917\t    gating_fail_reason_mmlu: γ CI includes zero, cal MAE ≈ raw MAE\n918\t    s3_constat_d005_auc_mean: 0.53\n919\t    s4_selfcritique_d005_auc_mean: 0.5\n920\t    s3_contribution_to_rrf: 0.55\n921\t    s4_contribution_to_rrf: 0.45\n922\t  result_rationale: 'The 2-signal mode is severely degraded because S1 (embedding\n923\t    similarity) carries nearly all discriminative power in the current framework.\n924\t\n925\t    (1) S3 ConStat AUC ≈ 0.53: ConStat measures the performance gap between original\n926\t    and paraphrased items. At d005, only 5% of items are contaminated, so the performance\n927\t    gap is small and noisy. ConStat also requires pre-existing paraphrases for evaluation\n928\t    — a practical limitation for deployment. At 0.5B, position bias (D045) confounds\n929\t    this signal further. At 3B, ConStat is somewhat better but still limited by the\n930\t    small paraphrase gap (~2.2pp mean from exp-003 item-aligned gap analysis).\n931\t\n932\t    (2) S4 Self-Critique AUC ≈ 0.50: Near chance. Self-Critique entropy collapse requires\n933\t    the model to reliably self-assess item familiarity — a metacognitive capability\n934\t    that small models (0.5B-3B) lack. At larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild improvement\n935\t    per exp-008 RL proxy results (delta ~0.08 MMLU), but this is insufficient for\n936\t    reliable discrimination.\n937\t\n938\t    (3) Combined 2-signal RRF AUC ≈ 0.52: Barely above chance. RRF of two near-chance\n939\t    signals does not meaningfully improve discrimination — the fusion preserves the\n940\t    best individual signal''s ranking, which is itself near-random.\n941\t\n942\t    (4) IRT with noisy exposure: The exposure scores fed to IRT are essentially noise.\n943\t    Consequences: (a) γ directions are partially preserved (ARC γ=-0.48, still negative)\n944\t    because the IRT model uses accuracy data and the prior to partially identify γ\n945\t    even without good exposure signals. But CIs are massive and all include zero.\n946\t    (b) MAEs worsen across the board: ARC from 0.028 to 0.049, approaching raw MAE\n947\t    (0.055). GSM8K MAE worsens from 0.006 to 0.012 (raw 0.006) — calibration with\n948\t    noise is WORSE than no calibration. (c) Coverage collapses: 0.40 (ARC) vs 0.67\n949\t    (4-signal) because noisy exposure scores produce poorly calibrated posteriors\n950\t    with wrong widths. (d) Kendall''s τ drops to 0.55 — barely better than random\n951\t    ranking.\n952\t\n953\t    (5) Calibration gating: ALL benchmarks FAIL gating (γ CI includes zero). The gating\n954\t    criterion correctly prevents misleading calibration — this is a FEATURE, not a\n955\t    failure. The paper frames 2-signal mode as demonstrating the gating criterion''s\n956\t    necessity and ContamCal''s honest self-assessment.\n957\t\n958\t    (6) Practical implication: Closed-weight model calibration is not currently viable\n959\t    with available API-level signals. S1 embedding similarity is the load-bearing\n960\t    component. This motivates future work on stronger API-compatible detection methods\n961\t    (output distribution analysis, zero-shot contamination detection, or model providers\n962\t    exposing limited embedding access).\n963\t\n964\t    '\n965\t  tables:\n966\t  - name: 'Table 9: Signal Mode Ablation — Calibration Quality'\n967\t    columns:\n968\t    - Mode\n969\t    - Signals\n970\t    - Mean AUC\n971\t    - ARC MAE\n972\t    - ARC Cov95\n973\t    - Kendall τ\n974\t    - Gating\n975\t    rows:\n976\t    - - S1-only (primary)\n977\t      - S1\n978\t      - 0.98\n979\t      - 0.028\n980\t      - 0.67\n981\t      - 0.82\n982\t      - 2/3 pass\n983\t    - - 4-signal RRF\n984\t      - S1+S2+S3+S4\n985\t      - 0.68\n986\t      - 0.03\n987\t      - 0.67\n988\t      - 0.82\n989\t      - 2/3 pass\n990\t    - - 2-signal (closed)\n991\t      - S3+S4\n992\t      - 0.52\n993\t      - 0.049\n994\t      - 0.4\n995\t      - 0.55\n996\t      - 0/3 pass\n997\t    - - No calibration\n998\t      - —\n999\t      - —\n1000\t      - 0.055\n1001\t      - —\n1002\t      - —\n1003\t      - —\n1004\t    note: '4-signal RRF ≈ S1-only because S2/S4 contribute minimal information. 2-signal\n1005\t      mode degrades below usefulness. Gating correctly prevents 2-signal calibration\n1006\t      from producing misleading results.\n1007\t\n1008\t      '\n1009\t  - name: 'Table S10: Per-Benchmark Gating Analysis (2-signal mode)'\n1010\t    columns:\n1011\t    - Benchmark\n1012\t    - γ̂\n1013\t    - CI excl 0?\n1014\t    - PPP\n1015\t    - cal<raw?\n1016\t    - Gating\n1017\t    rows:\n1018\t    - - ARC\n1019\t      - −0.48\n1020\t      - 'No'\n1021\t      - 0.35\n1022\t      - Yes (0.049<0.055)\n1023\t      - FAIL (γ not sig)\n1024\t    - - GSM8K\n1025\t      - '+0.06'\n1026\t      - 'No'\n1027\t      - 0.48\n1028\t      - No (0.012>0.006)\n1029\t      - FAIL (γ not sig + worse MAE)\n1030\t    - - MMLU\n1031\t      - '+0.28'\n1032\t      - 'No'\n1033\t      - 0.3\n1034\t      - No (0.014≈0.014)\n1035\t      - FAIL (γ not sig + no improvement)\n1036\t  figures_description:\n1037\t  - 'Figure S7: Three-panel bar chart comparing MAE, Coverage-95, and Kendall-τ across\n1038\t    signal modes (S1-only, 4-signal, 2-signal, raw). Clear monotonic degradation from\n1039\t    S1-only to 2-signal.'\n1040\t  - 'Figure S8: Exposure score distributions for 2-signal vs 4-signal RRF, showing\n1041\t    2-signal scores compressed near uniform (no discrimination) vs 4-signal bimodal\n1042\t    separation.'\n1043\t  analysis: 'This is an honest negative result that strengthens the paper. It demonstrates:\n1044\t    (1) ContamCal''s calibration quality degrades gracefully with signal quality —\n1045\t    not a fragile system that fails silently, but one that correctly self-assesses\n1046\t    via the gating criterion. (2) S1 embedding similarity is structurally necessary\n1047\t    for current-generation contamination detection in IRT. (3) The closed-weight gap\n1048\t    motivates future work on API-compatible detection signals. The paper notes that\n1049\t    at larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild differentiation potential (exp-008: delta≈0.08\n1050\t    MMLU Instruct vs SFT), suggesting 2-signal mode MAY become viable for closed-weight\n1051\t    models at frontier scale — flagged as future work.'\n1052\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02f57cba-a66d-4cc7-b317-dc071d8ba57e","uuid":"e1c2b679-117d-4e92-ba74-52b05a2bcbdf","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:42:44.147Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T155748/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'ContamCal characterization framework fully validated. Cross-family\n  Llama-3.2-3B replication confirms STEM gradient direction and ARC forgetting are\n  benchmark×domain properties, not Qwen-specific artifacts. 7B scale bridging completes\n  the scale modulation curve (catastrophic→negligible). Calibration conditionally\n  effective: ARC and MMLU-STEM pass gating, GSM8K correctly gated out. IRT outperforms\n  linear regression on ARC with bootstrap significance. QC-only d100 validates experimental\n  design. 72B ecological validity. Closed-weight 2-signal mode is an honest negative\n  result demonstrating S1-dependence. All 4 claims at full strength.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: sft_d100_qc_only\n  plan_id: exp-015\n  description: '100% dosage SFT using only 7547 QC-pass paraphrases (cosine>0.85,\n    BLEU<0.3) on Qwen2.5-0.5B. Compares against exp-001 d100-all (16697 items incl\n    QC-fail). Isolates noisy-paraphrase confound on signal quality and IRT estimation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    sft_steps: 3650\n    sft_final_loss: 1.555\n    sft_token_accuracy: 0.672\n    training_time_min: 60\n    mmlu_accuracy: 0.261\n    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.362\n    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.305\n    humaneval_pass1: 0.061\n    mmlu_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.028\n    gsm8k_delta_vs_d100_all: -0.003\n    arc_delta_vs_d100_all: 0.011\n    s1_auc_qconly_d100_vs_d000: 0.69\n    s1_auc_all_d100_vs_d000: 0.5\n    s1_auc_improvement: 0.19\n    gsm8k_gamma_qconly_refit: 0.08\n    gsm8k_gamma_qconly_ci:\n    - -0.85\n    - 1.0\n    gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    gsm8k_cal_mae_qconly: 0.032\n    gsm8k_cal_mae_all: 0.037\n  result_rationale: 'QC-only isolates two effects: signal quality and contamination\n    purity.\n\n    (1) Signal AUC improvement: At d100-all, S1 AUC collapses to ~0.50 because ALL\n    items are labeled \"contaminated\" but only ~47% (MMLU) to 60% (ARC) actually have\n    high-quality paraphrases. The signal can''t discriminate because labels are noisy.\n    QC-only removes the QC-fail items from both training data AND label set, so S1\n    now compares d000 (genuinely clean) vs d100-QC-only (genuinely contaminated on\n    the 7547 items). S1 AUC rises to ~0.69 — not 0.98 like d005 because at d100 the\n    model''s representations shift globally (not just on contaminated items), but\n    much better than d100-all''s labeling artifact.\n\n    (2) Accuracy differences: MMLU at 0.261 (vs d100-all 0.233) because fewer total\n    contaminated items means less position bias amplification at 0.5B. The 7547 QC-pass\n    items include 6596 MMLU items (47% of 14042) — still substantial MMLU contamination,\n    but the 5446 QC-fail items that were adding noise/capacity displacement are removed.\n    ARC at 0.305 (vs 0.294) similarly — 697/1172 QC-pass ARC items provide concentrated\n    forgetting signal but less total capacity displacement. GSM8K essentially unchanged\n    (0.362 vs 0.365) because only 228/1319 GSM8K items pass QC — negligible contamination\n    in either condition.\n\n    (3) IRT impact: GSM8K γ remains null (0.08) because open-ended format resists\n    contamination regardless of paraphrase quality. The QC-only condition mainly matters\n    for MC benchmarks, but those are excluded from 0.5B IRT per D045.\n\n    Paper value: validates that QC filtering improves signal discriminability. Reported\n    as supplementary evidence for experimental design robustness. The S1 AUC improvement\n    (0.50 → 0.69) directly addresses Concern #14 (d100 labeling artifact) by showing\n    the artifact is caused by noisy labels, not signal failure.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S1: QC-Only vs All-Paraphrase Comparison (0.5B d100)'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - d100-All\n    - d100-QC-Only\n    - Delta\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU Accuracy\n      - 0.233\n      - 0.261\n      - 0.028\n      - Less capacity displacement\n    - - ARC Accuracy\n      - 0.294\n      - 0.305\n      - 0.011\n      - Reduced noisy SFT interference\n    - - GSM8K Accuracy\n      - 0.365\n      - 0.362\n      - -0.003\n      - Null (minimal QC-pass GSM8K items)\n    - - S1 AUC (d100 vs d000)\n      - 0.5\n      - 0.69\n      - 0.19\n      - Label noise reduction\n    - - GSM8K Cal MAE\n      - 0.037\n      - 0.032\n      - -0.005\n      - Cleaner exposure → tighter IRT\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Supplementary: S1 score distribution histograms for d100-all vs d100-QC-only,\n    showing cleaner bimodal separation in QC-only condition'\n  analysis: 'Confirms the d100 AUC collapse (Concern #14) is a labeling artifact,\n    not signal degradation. QC filtering partially restores discriminability. However,\n    0.5B MC data remains excluded from main analysis per D045. Primary value is methodological\n    validation and supplementary evidence for the controlled injection design.\n\n    '\n- name: scale_bridging_7b\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Qwen2.5-7B LoRA SFT at 5 dosage levels (d000/d005/d025/d050/d100),\n    1 seed. Extends scale modulation to 7B. Multi-GPU LoRA on H100 (~4-8h per run).\n    Validates that contamination effects attenuate at larger scale.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    sft_time_per_run_hours: 6.5\n    total_gpu_hours: 32.5\n    lora_adapter_size_mb: 210\n    d000_mmlu: 0.724\n    d000_gsm8k: 0.793\n    d000_arc: 0.896\n    d000_humaneval: 0.268\n    d005_mmlu: 0.725\n    d005_gsm8k: 0.791\n    d005_arc: 0.895\n    d005_humaneval: 0.274\n    d025_mmlu: 0.726\n    d025_gsm8k: 0.795\n    d025_arc: 0.892\n    d025_humaneval: 0.28\n    d050_mmlu: 0.727\n    d050_gsm8k: 0.796\n    d050_arc: 0.89\n    d050_humaneval: 0.287\n    d100_mmlu: 0.729\n    d100_gsm8k: 0.8\n    d100_arc: 0.887\n    d100_humaneval: 0.293\n    mmlu_delta_d100: 0.005\n    gsm8k_delta_d100: 0.007\n    arc_delta_d100: -0.009\n    humaneval_delta_d100: 0.025\n    s1_d005_auc_7b: 0.97\n    s2_d005_auc_7b: 0.51\n    s2_d100_cohens_d_7b: -0.42\n    s4_d005_auc_7b: 0.53\n    arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.52\n    arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci:\n    - -0.12\n    - 1.18\n    arc_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl: 0.05\n    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci:\n    - -0.28\n    - 0.38\n    gsm8k_gamma_pooled_n32_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl: 0.58\n    mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci:\n    - -0.22\n    - 1.4\n    mmlu_gamma_pooled_n24_2pl_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    coverage_95_arc_n24: 0.625\n    coverage_95_gsm8k_n32: 0.688\n    coverage_95_mmlu_n24: 0.667\n    kendall_tau_arc_n24: 0.79\n    kendall_tau_gsm8k_n32: 0.83\n    scale_modulation:\n      arc_05b: -17.5\n      arc_15b: -0.7\n      arc_3b: -0.3\n      arc_7b: -0.9\n      mmlu_05b: -16.5\n      mmlu_15b: 0.8\n      mmlu_3b: 0.8\n      mmlu_7b: 0.5\n      gsm8k_05b: 0.1\n      gsm8k_15b: -0.5\n      gsm8k_3b: 0.2\n      gsm8k_7b: 0.7\n  result_rationale: 'At 7B, the model has sufficient capacity that LoRA SFT contamination\n    barely perturbs benchmark performance. The mechanism:\n\n    (1) MMLU shows <1pp change across dosages. The 7B model already encodes the factual\n    knowledge tested by MMLU; re-exposure via SFT paraphrases is redundant. The STEM\n    gradient (Math > Science >> CS_Eng) likely still exists at 7B but with attenuated\n    magnitudes (STEM_Math γ might be +0.3 instead of +0.97 at 0.5B-3B), making domain-level\n    γ harder to detect at 7B alone.\n\n    (2) ARC shows very mild forgetting (-0.9pp at d100). This is notable: the forgetting\n    DIRECTION persists at 7B (negative delta), confirming ARC forgetting is a benchmark\n    property not solely a small-model capacity artifact. But the magnitude is within\n    noise for a single seed. The 2PL+γ pooled fit at N=24 (1.5B+3B+7B, D045 excludes\n    0.5B) shows ARC γ=+0.52 — still positive because the very mild 7B forgetting is\n    offset by 3B near-zero effects. The NEGATIVE ARC γ from best tier targets requires\n    cross-family pooling (exp-014).\n\n    (3) GSM8K remains null (+0.7pp, within noise). Open-ended format resistance is\n    scale-independent, as predicted. The pooled N=32 2PL+γ narrows the CI ([-0.28,\n    0.38] vs [-0.36, 0.49] at N=24), further confirming the null.\n\n    (4) HumanEval shows moderate dose-response (+2.5pp at d100). At 7B, code generation\n    benefits from exposure to coding patterns in contamination data, but less dramatically\n    than at 1.5B (+17.7pp). This suggests HumanEval contamination sensitivity is also\n    scale-modulated but in the opposite direction from ARC — consistent with the \"benchmark-dependent\n    direction\" framing.\n\n    (5) Signal behavior: S1 remains reliable (AUC=0.97) because embedding similarity\n    is architecture- and scale-agnostic. S2 Min-K++ reversal attenuates at 7B (Cohen''s\n    d = -0.42 vs -1.98 at 0.5B) — larger models have more distributed representations,\n    so perplexity-based signals are less directionally misleading but still near-chance\n    for discrimination. S4 Self-Critique shows marginal improvement (0.53 vs 0.48\n    at 0.5B) — larger models can better self-assess, but the effect is modest.\n\n    (6) Coverage: Increasing N from 16→24 (ARC/MMLU) and 24→32 (GSM8K) provides moderate\n    improvement. GSM8K at N=32 reaches 0.688, approaching the best tier target of\n    0.60-0.75. ARC remains at 0.625 due to model specification issues (2PL+γ positive\n    γ). The best tier''s coverage_95_at_n24 ∈ [0.60, 0.75] is met by GSM8K and MMLU\n    but ARC needs cross-family data to resolve the γ sign.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: Scale Modulation — Accuracy Delta (d100 − d000)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - 0.5B\n    - 1.5B\n    - 3B\n    - 7B\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU*\n      - -16.5pp\n      - +0.8pp\n      - +0.8pp\n      - +0.5pp\n    - - ARC\n      - -17.5pp\n      - -0.7pp\n      - -0.3pp\n      - -0.9pp\n    - - GSM8K\n      - +0.1pp\n      - -0.5pp\n      - +0.2pp\n      - +0.7pp\n    - - HumanEval\n      - -11.0pp\n      - +17.7pp\n      - +0.6pp\n      - +2.5pp\n    note: '* 0.5B MMLU/ARC excluded from IRT (D045 position bias). ARC shows consistent\n      negative direction across all scales. GSM8K within noise at all scales. HumanEval\n      peaks at 1.5B.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table 4a: Pooled IRT 2PL+γ Results (Qwen-only, with 7B)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - N\n    - γ̂\n    - 95% CI\n    - Excl 0?\n    - PPP\n    - raw_MAE\n    - cal_MAE\n    rows:\n    - - ARC\n      - 24\n      - 0.52\n      - '[-0.12, 1.18]'\n      - 'No'\n      - 0.015\n      - 0.032\n      - 0.018\n    - - GSM8K\n      - 32\n      - 0.05\n      - '[-0.28, 0.38]'\n      - 'No'\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.155\n      - 0.005\n    - - MMLU\n      - 24\n      - 0.58\n      - '[-0.22, 1.40]'\n      - 'No'\n      - 0.21\n      - 0.014\n      - 0.006\n    note: 'ARC γ positive in Qwen-only pool (D045 excludes 0.5B forgetting). Cross-family\n      pooling (exp-014) needed for negative ARC γ per best tier.\n\n      '\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3: Dose-response curves across 4 scales (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B) for ARC, MMLU,\n    GSM8K — ARC converges to flat at ≥1.5B, GSM8K flat at all scales'\n  - 'Figure S2: Signal AUC heatmap extended to 7B — S1 stable, S2 reversal attenuates'\n  - 'Figure S3: 7B dose-response for all 4 benchmarks (near-flat lines confirming\n    scale modulation endpoint for MC)'\n  analysis: '7B completes the Qwen-only scale modulation story. Key contribution:\n    ARC forgetting DIRECTION persists at 7B (raw delta -0.9pp) even though the pooled\n    IRT γ is positive when 0.5B is excluded. This is resolved in exp-014: Llama cross-family\n    data where 3B may show stronger ARC forgetting, pulling pooled γ negative. The\n    7B data''s primary value is (a) validating negligible MC effects at scale, (b)\n    HumanEval dose-response at scale, (c) increasing pooled N for tighter CIs and\n    better coverage, (d) confirming S1 reliability and S2 reversal attenuation at\n    scale.\n\n    '\n- name: multi_family_validation\n  plan_id: exp-014\n  description: 'Controlled SFT injection on Llama-3.2-3B at 3 dosage levels (d000/d025/d100),\n    1 seed. Validates cross-family generalization of domain heterogeneity, ARC forgetting,\n    and GSM8K resistance. Critical for establishing contamination susceptibility as\n    benchmark×domain property.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    sft_time_per_run_hours: 2.3\n    total_gpu_hours: 6.9\n    llama_d000_mmlu: 0.455\n    llama_d000_gsm8k: 0.481\n    llama_d000_arc: 0.742\n    llama_d000_humaneval: 0.195\n    llama_d025_mmlu: 0.459\n    llama_d025_gsm8k: 0.488\n    llama_d025_arc: 0.721\n    llama_d025_humaneval: 0.207\n    llama_d100_mmlu: 0.463\n    llama_d100_gsm8k: 0.495\n    llama_d100_arc: 0.695\n    llama_d100_humaneval: 0.22\n    llama_arc_delta: -0.047\n    llama_mmlu_delta: 0.008\n    llama_gsm8k_delta: 0.014\n    llama_humaneval_delta: 0.025\n    llama_s1_d005_auc: 0.96\n    llama_s2_d005_auc: 0.49\n    llama_s2_d100_cohens_d: -1.45\n    llama_s4_d005_auc: 0.5\n    llama_arc_gamma: -1.65\n    llama_arc_gamma_ci:\n    - -3.8\n    - 0.5\n    llama_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    llama_gsm8k_gamma: 0.12\n    llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n    - -1.3\n    - 1.5\n    llama_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    llama_stem_math_gamma: 0.73\n    llama_stem_math_ci:\n    - 0.08\n    - 1.42\n    llama_stem_math_ci_excludes_zero: true\n    llama_stem_science_gamma: 0.56\n    llama_stem_science_ci:\n    - -0.12\n    - 1.25\n    llama_stem_science_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    llama_cs_eng_gamma: 0.08\n    llama_cs_eng_ci:\n    - -0.65\n    - 0.82\n    llama_cs_eng_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    stem_gradient_direction_matches_qwen: true\n    arc_forgetting_direction_matches_qwen: true\n    gsm8k_null_matches_qwen: true\n    pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl: -1.18\n    pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci:\n    - -2.25\n    - -0.15\n    pooled_cross_family_arc_gamma_4pl_ci_excludes_zero: true\n    pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma: 0.08\n    pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.22\n    - 0.38\n    pooled_cross_family_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    pooled_cross_family_n_models: 30\n    coverage_95_cross_family_arc: 0.67\n    coverage_95_cross_family_gsm8k: 0.7\n    kendall_tau_cross_family: 0.82\n    irt_mae_arc: 0.028\n    linear_reg_mae_arc: 0.048\n    bootstrap_p_irt_vs_linear: 0.032\n    bootstrap_n: 5000\n    benchmarks_passing_gating: 2\n    gating_pass_arc: true\n    gating_pass_mmlu_stem: true\n    gating_fail_gsm8k: true\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-family validation is the most consequential planned experiment.\n    It elevates the characterization framework from \"Qwen-specific observation\" to\n    \"general benchmark property.\" Here''s why each result is expected:\n\n    (1) Llama-3.2-3B ARC forgetting (-4.7pp at d100): Llama-3.2-3B has a lower ARC\n    baseline (0.742 vs Qwen2.5-3B 0.824) and a different architecture (grouped-query\n    attention, different tokenizer). The lower baseline means the model has less headroom\n    — SFT contamination competes with limited capacity for science reasoning, producing\n    more pronounced forgetting than Qwen2.5-3B (-0.3pp). This is the critical mechanism:\n    at equivalent parameter count, models with less task-specific capacity show stronger\n    forgetting, and the forgetting DIRECTION is preserved regardless of architecture.\n    The raw -4.7pp delta provides direct evidence for ARC forgetting as a benchmark\n    property.\n\n    (2) Llama-only IRT (N=6): γ=-1.65 but wide CI crossing zero. N=6 is too small\n    for significance — this is expected from exp-004 simulations showing N≥50 for\n    reliable recovery. However, the point estimate''s SIGN is negative, directionally\n    consistent with Qwen results. The value is the sign, not the significance.\n\n    (3) Pooled cross-family 4PL+γ (N=30): This is where best tier is achieved. Adding\n    Llama data (with genuine ARC forgetting) to Qwen data (with near-flat ARC at 1.5B+/3B)\n    produces a negative pooled γ=-1.18 because the Llama points pull the estimate\n    negative while 7B and 3B Qwen points sit near zero. The 4PL+γ model (appropriate\n    for MCQ) with S1-only exposure (the reliable signal) produces CI=[-2.25, -0.15]\n    that excludes zero. This is within the best tier target range of [-2.5, -0.8].\n\n    (4) STEM gradient replication: Llama STEM_Math γ=0.73 (Qwen: 0.97) — same positive\n    direction, slightly smaller magnitude. The gradient Math > Science > CS_Eng is\n    preserved: 0.73 > 0.56 > 0.08. Math CI excludes zero; Science borderline; CS_Eng\n    null. The direction match across families (both showing Math > Science >> CS_Eng\n    ≈ 0) is strong evidence that contamination susceptibility maps to factual knowledge\n    density, a property of the benchmark items, not the model. Lower Llama magnitudes\n    reflect: (a) different pre-training distribution (less MMLU-adjacent data in Llama''s\n    pre-training), (b) different SFT dynamics (Llama''s architecture processes paraphrases\n    differently), but (c) the same underlying susceptibility gradient determined by\n    item characteristics.\n\n    (5) GSM8K null replication: γ=0.12, CI firmly crossing zero. Open-ended format\n    resistance is architecture-independent because the mechanism is format-driven:\n    chain-of-thought generation cannot be boosted by memorizing answer templates.\n    This holds across model families.\n\n    (6) Signal behavior: S1 embedding AUC=0.96 (Llama) — sentence-transformer similarity\n    is model-agnostic, confirming S1''s robustness. S2 Min-K++ reversal replicates\n    (Cohen''s d=-1.45) because the reversal is driven by small-model memorization\n    dynamics that alter perplexity in the opposite-to-expected direction, a phenomenon\n    shared across architectures at comparable scale.\n\n    (7) IRT vs linear regression (bootstrap p=0.032): With cross-family N=30 and the\n    4PL+γ model, ARC IRT MAE=0.028 vs linear regression MAE=0.048. The bootstrap test\n    is significant because IRT captures the nonlinear relationship between ability,\n    contamination, and item difficulty that linear regression misses — specifically,\n    the c/d parameters of 4PL model the guessing and ceiling effects in MCQ that produce\n    heteroscedastic residuals. At N=30, the IRT advantage is large enough to be statistically\n    distinguishable.\n\n    (8) Calibration gating: ARC passes (γ significant, PPP in range, cal MAE < raw\n    MAE). MMLU-STEM domains pass at the domain level (STEM_Math γ significant across\n    both families). GSM8K correctly gated out (γ null). This yields 2 benchmarks (of\n    3 main) passing gating, within the best tier target of 2-3.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: Cross-Family MMLU Domain γ Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Domain\n    - Qwen γ̂ (N=23)\n    - Llama γ̂ (N=6)\n    - Direction Match\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - STEM_Math\n      - +0.97 [0.53,1.43]\n      - +0.73 [0.08,1.42]\n      - ✓ both positive\n      - Factual recall — both susceptible\n    - - STEM_Science\n      - +0.84 [0.39,1.27]\n      - +0.56 [−0.12,1.25]\n      - ✓ both positive*\n      - Science facts — susceptible, Llama borderline\n    - - STEM_CS_Eng\n      - +0.20 [−0.45,0.84]\n      - +0.08 [−0.65,0.82]\n      - ✓ both null\n      - Procedural — resistant\n    - - Medical\n      - −0.20 PPP=1.0\n      - −0.15 PPP=1.0\n      - ✓ both misfit\n      - 2PL+γ scope boundary\n    - - Humanities\n      - −0.41 PPP=1.0\n      - −0.30 PPP=1.0\n      - ✓ both misfit\n      - 2PL+γ scope boundary\n    note: '* Llama STEM_Science CI borderline includes zero at N=6.'\n  - name: 'Table 6: Cross-Family Benchmark γ Summary'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Qwen-only 2PL+γ (N=24)\n    - Llama-only 2PL+γ (N=6)\n    - Pooled 4PL+γ S1 (N=30)\n    - Direction\n    rows:\n    - - ARC\n      - +0.52 [−0.12,1.18]\n      - −1.65 [−3.8,0.5]\n      - −1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]*\n      - Forgetting (benchmark)\n    - - GSM8K\n      - +0.05 [−0.28,0.38]\n      - +0.12 [−1.3,1.5]\n      - +0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\n      - Null (format)\n    - - MMLU\n      - +0.58 [−0.22,1.40]\n      - +0.35 [−0.8,1.5]\n      - +0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\n      - Domain-dependent\n    note: '* ARC γ significant only in pooled cross-family 4PL+γ — this is the primary\n      specification for MCQ.'\n  - name: 'Table 7: IRT vs Linear Regression (ARC, N=30 cross-family)'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - MAE\n    - 95% Bootstrap CI\n    - p vs Linear\n    rows:\n    - - Raw (no adjustment)\n      - 0.055\n      - '[0.038, 0.072]'\n      - —\n    - - B4 Linear Regression\n      - 0.048\n      - '[0.032, 0.064]'\n      - —\n    - - ContamCal 4PL+γ\n      - 0.028\n      - '[0.015, 0.041]'\n      - p=0.032\n    - - B3 Vanilla 4PL\n      - 0.062\n      - '[0.043, 0.081]'\n      - p=0.92\n    - - B6 DCR Fuzzy\n      - 0.185\n      - '[0.140, 0.230]'\n      - p<0.001\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5: Paired forest plot — domain-level γ for Qwen vs Llama, showing directional\n    consistency. STEM domains (Math, Science) positive; non-STEM PPP=1.0 boundary.\n    Error bars overlap but signs match.'\n  - 'Figure 6: Cross-family ARC dose-response — Llama shows clearer negative slope\n    than Qwen at 3B, converging evidence for ARC forgetting as benchmark property.'\n  - 'Figure S4: MMLU STEM gradient visualization (γ vs domain) for both families on\n    same axes, showing parallel gradients at different magnitudes.'\n  analysis: 'This experiment is the linchpin of the best tier. It resolves the ARC\n    γ sign tension: Qwen-only (D045-compliant, excluding 0.5B) shows positive γ because\n    Qwen2.5-3B is barely affected on ARC; Llama-3.2-3B shows genuine forgetting (-4.7pp),\n    pulling the cross-family pooled γ negative. The 4PL+γ specification (appropriate\n    for MCQ) captures the guessing dynamics that 2PL misses. At N=30 cross-family,\n    ARC γ CI excludes zero at [-2.25, -0.15], meeting best tier targets.\n\n    The STEM gradient replication across families is the strongest evidence for the\n    characterization framework: contamination susceptibility is determined by item-level\n    properties (factual knowledge density), not model-level properties (architecture,\n    tokenizer, pre-training data). Two families, same gradient direction, same benchmark-dependent\n    effects = benchmark×domain property established.\n\n    IRT vs linear regression achieves statistical significance (p=0.032) because N=30\n    provides sufficient power and the 4PL nonlinear modeling of MCQ guessing/ceiling\n    effects captures variance that linear regression cannot. This addresses Concern\n    #21.\n\n    '\n- name: rl_observational_case_study\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'Apply ContamCal pipeline to existing RL-trained models: Qwen2.5-Instruct\n    (0.5B/1.5B/3B/7B), DeepSeek-R1-Distill (1.5B/7B). Signal computation, IRT fitting\n    with wide priors, expected regret quantification, and near-zero γ specificity\n    check on known-clean benchmark.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    instruct_05b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.58\n    instruct_05b_s1_auc_arc: 0.55\n    instruct_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.61\n    instruct_15b_s1_auc_arc: 0.6\n    instruct_3b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.63\n    instruct_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.65\n    deepseek_r1_15b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.57\n    deepseek_r1_7b_s1_auc_mmlu: 0.62\n    instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_mmlu: 0.078\n    instruct_s4_vs_sft_delta_arc: 0.031\n    obs_arc_gamma: -0.85\n    obs_arc_gamma_ci:\n    - -2.1\n    - 0.4\n    obs_arc_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    obs_gsm8k_gamma: 0.18\n    obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.35\n    - 0.72\n    obs_gsm8k_gamma_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    obs_mmlu_gamma: 0.42\n    obs_mmlu_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.25\n    - 1.1\n    regret_uncalibrated_pp: 3.4\n    regret_calibrated_pp: 1.9\n    regret_remove_rescore_pp: 2.8\n    regret_reduction_pct: 44.1\n    regret_n_candidates: 12\n    regret_top_k: 5\n    regret_ground_truth: held-out clean benchmark (not calibrated scores)\n    clean_benchmark_name: WinoGrande\n    clean_gamma: 0.04\n    clean_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.55\n    - 0.62\n    clean_gamma_ci_includes_zero: true\n    specificity_confirmed: true\n    coverage_95_obs: 0.53\n    coverage_95_controlled_only: 0.67\n    deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_15b: 0.863\n    deepseek_r1_gsm8k_accuracy_7b: 0.924\n    deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_15b: 0.542\n    deepseek_r1_mmlu_accuracy_7b: 0.718\n  result_rationale: 'Observational RL analysis necessarily produces wider CIs and\n    weaker γ estimates. The key contributions are directional consistency, practical\n    utility, and specificity validation.\n\n    (1) Signal degradation: S1 AUC drops from 0.95-0.99 (controlled SFT) to 0.55-0.65\n    (observational RL). This is expected because: (a) we don''t know which items these\n    models saw during training, so S1''s reference database (original benchmark items)\n    may not match the actual contamination source, (b) RL post-training (RLHF/DPO)\n    modifies representations beyond what SFT alone does, reducing embedding similarity\n    between model outputs and the benchmark items we check against, (c) Instruct models\n    are further from base than our controlled SFT models, making S1 similarity scores\n    noisier. S4 Self-Critique shows the RL-sensitivity found in exp-008 (delta ~0.08\n    on MMLU), confirming that S4 differentiates SFT from RL to a small degree.\n\n    (2) IRT γ directions: ARC γ=-0.85 (negative, directionally consistent with controlled\n    results). The magnitude is attenuated because observational exposure scores are\n    noisier. CI includes zero because N_observational is small and signal quality\n    is lower. GSM8K γ=0.18 (null, consistent). MMLU γ=0.42 (positive direction, consistent\n    with STEM inflation). The CIs are uniformly wider than controlled experiments\n    — this is honest and expected.\n\n    (3) Expected regret: Even with noisy signals, calibrated ranking reduces expected\n    regret by 44% for top-5 model selection. The mechanism: IRT calibration correctly\n    adjusts the ranking of models with high exposure scores (likely contaminated)\n    relative to models with low exposure. Even with wide CIs, the DIRECTION of adjustment\n    is correct often enough to improve selection. Ground truth = held-out clean benchmark\n    (WinoGrande) scores, NOT calibrated scores, avoiding circularity (Concern #12).\n    The 44% reduction is meaningful for practitioners: choosing among top-5 models\n    with calibrated vs uncalibrated ranking avoids ~1.5pp expected contamination bias.\n\n    (4) Specificity: WinoGrande γ=0.04, CI firmly crossing zero. This benchmark is\n    not in standard contamination pathways (sentence completion format, less likely\n    to appear in training data). The near-zero γ confirms ContamCal doesn''t hallucinate\n    contamination effects — it produces null results when contamination is absent.\n    This addresses the implicit concern that IRT γ might absorb any model variation,\n    not specifically contamination.\n\n    (5) DeepSeek-R1-Distill: These models show high GSM8K accuracy (0.863-0.924) potentially\n    reflecting distillation from R1''s chain-of-thought, which may encode benchmark-specific\n    patterns. However, without controlled injection, we cannot distinguish genuine\n    capability from contamination. The IRT framework assigns wide CIs, correctly reflecting\n    this uncertainty.\n\n    (6) Coverage at 0.53 (observational pooled): Lower than controlled-only (0.67)\n    because observational data introduces heterogeneity that the IRT model doesn''t\n    fully capture. This is reported honestly as a limitation of observational extension.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 8: Expected Regret — Top-5 Model Selection (N=12 candidates)'\n    columns:\n    - Strategy\n    - Expected Regret (pp)\n    - vs Uncalibrated\n    - Description\n    rows:\n    - - Uncalibrated (raw ranking)\n      - 3.4\n      - —\n      - Rank by raw benchmark scores\n    - - Remove-and-rescore\n      - 2.8\n      - −17.6%\n      - Drop flagged items, re-rank\n    - - ContamCal (calibrated)\n      - 1.9\n      - −44.1%\n      - IRT-adjusted scores, re-rank\n    - - Oracle (ground truth)\n      - 0.0\n      - −100%\n      - Perfect clean scores\n  - name: 'Table S7: Observational vs Controlled γ Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Controlled γ̂ (N=30)\n    - w/ Observational γ̂ (N≈38)\n    - Direction Consistent\n    rows:\n    - - ARC\n      - −1.18 [−2.25,−0.15]\n      - −0.85 [−2.1,0.4]\n      - ✓ (attenuated, CI wider)\n    - - GSM8K\n      - +0.08 [−0.22,0.38]\n      - +0.18 [−0.35,0.72]\n      - ✓ (null preserved)\n    - - MMLU\n      - +0.52 [−0.18,1.22]\n      - +0.42 [−0.25,1.10]\n      - ✓ (positive preserved)\n  - name: 'Table S8: Specificity Check — Known-Clean Benchmark'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - γ̂\n    - 95% CI\n    - Includes 0?\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - WinoGrande\n      - 0.04\n      - '[−0.55, 0.62]'\n      - 'Yes'\n      - No false contamination detection\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 7: Expected regret curve — regret vs number of selected models (top-k)\n    for 3 strategies, showing consistent calibration advantage across k=3,5,7,10.'\n  - 'Figure S5: Observational γ forest plot overlaid on controlled γ estimates. Direction\n    consistent, CIs wider. Gray band = controlled 95% CI.'\n  analysis: 'The RL observational case study provides three key contributions: (1)\n    directional consistency confirms the characterization framework generalizes beyond\n    controlled SFT, (2) expected regret reduction demonstrates practical value even\n    with noisy signals, (3) specificity check validates that ContamCal produces null\n    results on clean benchmarks. The wider CIs and non-significance of individual\n    γ estimates are honest — we frame this as \"directional external validity\" not\n    \"quantitative confirmation.\" DeepSeek-R1 results motivate future work on RL-contamination\n    interaction. Coverage degradation (0.67→0.53) when adding observational data is\n    a limitation, attributed to heterogeneity from mixing controlled and observational\n    designs.\n\n    '\n- name: observational_72b\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: 'Qwen2.5-72B inference-only observational analysis. Compute signals\n    and benchmark evaluations; add as single high-ability anchor point in pooled IRT.\n    No SFT. Extends ecological validity to frontier scale.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mmlu_accuracy: 0.848\n    gsm8k_accuracy: 0.916\n    arc_challenge_accuracy: 0.944\n    humaneval_pass1: 0.628\n    s1_exposure_mmlu: 0.11\n    s1_exposure_arc: 0.07\n    s1_exposure_gsm8k: 0.14\n    s2_minkpp_mmlu_mean: 1.69\n    s2_minkpp_arc_mean: 1.68\n    s4_selfcritique_mmlu_entropy: 2.35\n    pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma: -1.15\n    pooled_with_72b_arc_gamma_ci:\n    - -2.2\n    - -0.12\n    pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma: 0.07\n    pooled_with_72b_gsm8k_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.21\n    - 0.36\n    pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma: 0.5\n    pooled_with_72b_mmlu_gamma_ci:\n    - -0.16\n    - 1.18\n    pooled_with_72b_n: 31\n    arc_72b_raw: 0.944\n    arc_72b_calibrated: 0.948\n    arc_72b_delta: 0.004\n    mmlu_72b_raw: 0.848\n    mmlu_72b_calibrated: 0.843\n    mmlu_72b_delta: -0.005\n    gsm8k_72b_raw: 0.916\n    gsm8k_72b_calibrated: 0.916\n    gsm8k_72b_delta: 0.0\n    coverage_95_with_72b: 0.68\n    kendall_tau_with_72b: 0.84\n    inference_gpu_hours: 12\n  result_rationale: 'At 72B, the model is near ceiling on most benchmarks, and contamination\n    effects — if any exist from pre-training data overlap — are undetectable.\n\n    (1) Accuracy near ceiling: 72B achieves 84.8% MMLU, 94.4% ARC, 91.6% GSM8K. These\n    are near the practical ceiling for these benchmarks (human performance is ~89.8%\n    MMLU, ~80% GSM8K-hard). At this performance level, any contamination inflation\n    is small relative to genuine capability.\n\n    (2) Low exposure scores: S1 exposure scores are 0.07-0.14 (vs 0.0-0.20 range in\n    controlled SFT models). The 72B model''s training data likely includes some benchmark-adjacent\n    material through natural web crawl, producing mild S1 similarity. But these scores\n    are comparable to d000 (clean) controlled models, so the IRT model correctly treats\n    72B as a low-exposure data point.\n\n    (3) Negligible calibration deltas: ARC +0.4pp (mild positive correction from the\n    forgetting model — the IRT model slightly \"un-forgets\" for 72B, consistent with\n    the negative γ meaning lower-exposure models have slightly higher true ability\n    than observed). MMLU -0.5pp (mild STEM inflation correction consistent with positive\n    MMLU γ). GSM8K unchanged (null γ means no adjustment).\n\n    (4) Value as high-θ anchor: The IRT model benefits from a wider ability distribution\n    (0.5B low-ability through 72B high-ability). The 72B data point helps anchor item\n    difficulty parameters at the high end, improving estimation precision for all\n    models. This is analogous to including very high-ability examinees in educational\n    testing — they provide information about difficult items that lower-ability examinees\n    cannot. Kendall''s τ improves from 0.82 to 0.84 due to better separation at the\n    high end of the ability distribution.\n\n    (5) Coverage marginal improvement: 0.67→0.68 (adding 1 data point to N≈30 has\n    minimal impact on coverage). The value is not statistical power but ecological\n    validity at frontier scale.\n\n    (6) Inference cost: ~12 GPU-hours for evaluating 72B on 4 benchmarks plus computing\n    3 signals on all items. Dominated by S2 Min-K++ which requires full forward passes\n    on ~16K items.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table S9: 72B Observational Calibration'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - Raw Accuracy\n    - Calibrated\n    - Δ (pp)\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - MMLU\n      - 0.848\n      - 0.843\n      - −0.5\n      - Mild STEM inflation correction\n    - - ARC\n      - 0.944\n      - 0.948\n      - '+0.4'\n      - Forgetting model slight upward correction\n    - - GSM8K\n      - 0.916\n      - 0.916\n      - '0.0'\n      - Null γ → no adjustment\n    - - HumanEval\n      - 0.628\n      - 0.628\n      - '0.0'\n      - Not calibrated (appendix-only)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S6: Calibrated-vs-raw accuracy scatter across all scales (0.5B to 72B).\n    72B cluster on the diagonal — minimal correction. Dashed line = identity. Lower\n    scales show larger deviations.'\n  - 'Inset in Figure 3: 72B point added to dose-response plot at d=unknown, positioned\n    at observed accuracy with wide CI bar.'\n  analysis: '72B confirms the scale modulation endpoint: at frontier scale, contamination\n    effects vanish for all practical purposes. The sub-1pp calibration deltas are\n    within evaluation noise. The contribution is ecological validity (\"the framework\n    applies from 0.5B to 72B\") and the high-θ anchor effect on IRT estimation. Paper\n    frames as: \"At frontier scales, raw benchmark scores are already reliable estimates\n    of true ability — ContamCal''s value concentrates at smaller scales where contamination\n    effects are measurable.\"\n\n    '\n- name: closed_weight_2signal_mode\n  plan_id: exp-013\n  description: 'Ablation: 2-signal closed-weight mode using only ConStat (S3, performance\n    gap between original and paraphrased items) and Self-Critique (S4, entropy collapse).\n    No embeddings (S1) or logprobs (S2 Min-K++). Quantifies calibration degradation\n    when only API-level signals are available.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mmlu: 0.52\n    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_arc: 0.53\n    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_gsm8k: 0.51\n    rrf_2signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.52\n    rrf_4signal_auc_d005_mean: 0.68\n    rrf_s1only_auc_d005_mean: 0.98\n    arc_gamma_2signal: -0.48\n    arc_gamma_2signal_ci:\n    - -2.4\n    - 1.5\n    arc_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    gsm8k_gamma_2signal: 0.06\n    gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci:\n    - -1.0\n    - 1.1\n    gsm8k_gamma_2signal_ci_excludes_zero: false\n    mmlu_gamma_2signal: 0.28\n    mmlu_gamma_2signal_ci:\n    - -1.2\n    - 1.8\n    arc_mae_2signal: 0.049\n    arc_mae_4signal_s1only: 0.028\n    arc_mae_raw: 0.055\n    gsm8k_mae_2signal: 0.012\n    gsm8k_mae_raw: 0.006\n    mmlu_mae_2signal: 0.014\n    mmlu_mae_raw: 0.014\n    coverage_95_2signal_arc: 0.4\n    coverage_95_2signal_gsm8k: 0.47\n    coverage_95_4signal_arc: 0.67\n    coverage_95_4signal_gsm8k: 0.7\n    kendall_tau_2signal: 0.55\n    kendall_tau_4signal: 0.82\n    benchmarks_passing_gating_2signal: 0\n    gating_fail_reason_arc: γ CI includes zero\n    gating_fail_reason_gsm8k: γ CI includes zero (expected)\n    gating_fail_reason_mmlu: γ CI includes zero, cal MAE ≈ raw MAE\n    s3_constat_d005_auc_mean: 0.53\n    s4_selfcritique_d005_auc_mean: 0.5\n    s3_contribution_to_rrf: 0.55\n    s4_contribution_to_rrf: 0.45\n  result_rationale: 'The 2-signal mode is severely degraded because S1 (embedding\n    similarity) carries nearly all discriminative power in the current framework.\n\n    (1) S3 ConStat AUC ≈ 0.53: ConStat measures the performance gap between original\n    and paraphrased items. At d005, only 5% of items are contaminated, so the performance\n    gap is small and noisy. ConStat also requires pre-existing paraphrases for evaluation\n    — a practical limitation for deployment. At 0.5B, position bias (D045) confounds\n    this signal further. At 3B, ConStat is somewhat better but still limited by the\n    small paraphrase gap (~2.2pp mean from exp-003 item-aligned gap analysis).\n\n    (2) S4 Self-Critique AUC ≈ 0.50: Near chance. Self-Critique entropy collapse requires\n    the model to reliably self-assess item familiarity — a metacognitive capability\n    that small models (0.5B-3B) lack. At larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild improvement\n    per exp-008 RL proxy results (delta ~0.08 MMLU), but this is insufficient for\n    reliable discrimination.\n\n    (3) Combined 2-signal RRF AUC ≈ 0.52: Barely above chance. RRF of two near-chance\n    signals does not meaningfully improve discrimination — the fusion preserves the\n    best individual signal''s ranking, which is itself near-random.\n\n    (4) IRT with noisy exposure: The exposure scores fed to IRT are essentially noise.\n    Consequences: (a) γ directions are partially preserved (ARC γ=-0.48, still negative)\n    because the IRT model uses accuracy data and the prior to partially identify γ\n    even without good exposure signals. But CIs are massive and all include zero.\n    (b) MAEs worsen across the board: ARC from 0.028 to 0.049, approaching raw MAE\n    (0.055). GSM8K MAE worsens from 0.006 to 0.012 (raw 0.006) — calibration with\n    noise is WORSE than no calibration. (c) Coverage collapses: 0.40 (ARC) vs 0.67\n    (4-signal) because noisy exposure scores produce poorly calibrated posteriors\n    with wrong widths. (d) Kendall''s τ drops to 0.55 — barely better than random\n    ranking.\n\n    (5) Calibration gating: ALL benchmarks FAIL gating (γ CI includes zero). The gating\n    criterion correctly prevents misleading calibration — this is a FEATURE, not a\n    failure. The paper frames 2-signal mode as demonstrating the gating criterion''s\n    necessity and ContamCal''s honest self-assessment.\n\n    (6) Practical implication: Closed-weight model calibration is not currently viable\n    with available API-level signals. S1 embedding similarity is the load-bearing\n    component. This motivates future work on stronger API-compatible detection methods\n    (output distribution analysis, zero-shot contamination detection, or model providers\n    exposing limited embedding access).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 9: Signal Mode Ablation — Calibration Quality'\n    columns:\n    - Mode\n    - Signals\n    - Mean AUC\n    - ARC MAE\n    - ARC Cov95\n    - Kendall τ\n    - Gating\n    rows:\n    - - S1-only (primary)\n      - S1\n      - 0.98\n      - 0.028\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.82\n      - 2/3 pass\n    - - 4-signal RRF\n      - S1+S2+S3+S4\n      - 0.68\n      - 0.03\n      - 0.67\n      - 0.82\n      - 2/3 pass\n    - - 2-signal (closed)\n      - S3+S4\n      - 0.52\n      - 0.049\n      - 0.4\n      - 0.55\n      - 0/3 pass\n    - - No calibration\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0.055\n      - —\n      - —\n      - —\n    note: '4-signal RRF ≈ S1-only because S2/S4 contribute minimal information. 2-signal\n      mode degrades below usefulness. Gating correctly prevents 2-signal calibration\n      from producing misleading results.\n\n      '\n  - name: 'Table S10: Per-Benchmark Gating Analysis (2-signal mode)'\n    columns:\n    - Benchmark\n    - γ̂\n    - CI excl 0?\n    - PPP\n    - cal<raw?\n    - Gating\n    rows:\n    - - ARC\n      - −0.48\n      - 'No'\n      - 0.35\n      - Yes (0.049<0.055)\n      - FAIL (γ not sig)\n    - - GSM8K\n      - '+0.06'\n      - 'No'\n      - 0.48\n      - No (0.012>0.006)\n      - FAIL (γ not sig + worse MAE)\n    - - MMLU\n      - '+0.28'\n      - 'No'\n      - 0.3\n      - No (0.014≈0.014)\n      - FAIL (γ not sig + no improvement)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure S7: Three-panel bar chart comparing MAE, Coverage-95, and Kendall-τ across\n    signal modes (S1-only, 4-signal, 2-signal, raw). Clear monotonic degradation from\n    S1-only to 2-signal.'\n  - 'Figure S8: Exposure score distributions for 2-signal vs 4-signal RRF, showing\n    2-signal scores compressed near uniform (no discrimination) vs 4-signal bimodal\n    separation.'\n  analysis: 'This is an honest negative result that strengthens the paper. It demonstrates:\n    (1) ContamCal''s calibration quality degrades gracefully with signal quality —\n    not a fragile system that fails silently, but one that correctly self-assesses\n    via the gating criterion. (2) S1 embedding similarity is structurally necessary\n    for current-generation contamination detection in IRT. (3) The closed-weight gap\n    motivates future work on API-compatible detection signals. The paper notes that\n    at larger scales (7B+), S4 shows mild differentiation potential (exp-008: delta≈0.08\n    MMLU Instruct vs SFT), suggesting 2-signal mode MAY become viable for closed-weight\n    models at frontier scale — flagged as future work.'\n","numLines":1052,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1052}}}
